ack plays d4 if black plays  something like e7 or sorry bishop e6  then pawn to d4 and white is going to  enjoy a space advantage  nice pawn chain something that uh this  pawn structure is like a  winner or a french so black has to play  d4 um to avoid this  uh d4 is a bit of a slow move from black  though after pawn to d4  white punishes black for their slow play  by playing queen to g3  and it makes it extremely difficult for  black to develop their bishop  and here there's two different move  orders to get to the same position  but knight c6 is the more popular move  order here after knight c6 there's  bishop to e2  this bishop is going to make its way to  f3  it's very important that you don't put  the knight on f3 that is the most  important diagonal for this bishop  and you have to bring the bishop there  first because otherwise if knight e2 you  just block the path for the bishop  bishop e2 now bishop e6 developing  bishop to f3 queen d7 and now black is  prepared to castle queenside  knight to e2 and now pawn takes c3  we can let this sit here as white this  pawn will be a target for a while  so instead of focusing on taking this  pawn back right away we should continue  developing the pieces  bishop to e3 knight to b4  attacking both pawns he had to go rook  to c1 to save the pawn  and here black is slightly better  for some more beginner players you might  be wondering why they can't take on a2  and the reason for this is uh bishop  takes b7  and white will get some counter play add  a rook to b8  queen to f3 protecting the bishop and  you can sacrifice this  uh this rook here because of this  pin you will pin the queen to the king  winning the queen  and having an interesting material  imbalance  so that is another way for black to play  they can play f5  but the main line here after queen to f3  is  knight takes c3 and there's multiple  ways for white to capture  but the the capture i'm going to  recommend here is b takes c3  and again the idea is you're going to  play d4 get this nice space advantage  have a nice strong pawn sensor  easy development so bishop e7 getting  ready to castle king side  pawn to d4 and now castles  from black black can also try for an  early c5 but if they play c5 before they  castle  white can just go bishop d3 pawn to c4  this bishop goes to f5 and trades itself  off  queen d7 trade the queens off and after  knight e2  we enter this end game where while  material is equal  you know white has this space advantage  it's pretty easy to get the pieces out  like a4 bishop a3  the knight can go to f4 and hit d5  so that end game uh could be equal for  both sides by  i feel like white stands slightly better  so black should go for castles  and now bishop to d3  after bishop to d3 we have um three very  interesting continuations  uh the purpose of bishop to d3 is to put  pressure on h7  and we'll show why this is important so  let's say black plays  what looks to be a natural move looks  like bishop e6  is completely fine you're protecting f7  protecting your center this is great  however this introduces the idea of  queen to h5  and this may look fine at first you know  you can play g6  stop the mate threat queen goes to h6  you might have ideas of bishop to g5 and  f6 in the future  you can't do that right now because of  the queen and bishop but you're going to  run the h  pawn down sacrifice the h pawn with some  big mate threats something resembling  the french defense gone wrong in the  winor  or some ideas in the dragon with the  fiancee bishop you're going to run this  h-pawn down  and sacrifice it so  black's best idea is to try and kick  this queen out right away  so they do it with rook to e8 and then  after knight f3 bishop to f8  now queen to f4 more pressure on f7 and  after c5 knight to g5 white does have a  very promising position the computer is  putting it uh white at about plus one  and a half  some positions plus two and again the  idea is just rush your h-pawn down  and rip open black's king side  instead of playing bishop to e6 one try  black has here which can lead to a  forced  draw is pawn to f6  so pawn to f6 here is an idea that you  can see in the french sometimes whenever  they try and attack  the head of the pawn chain trying to rip  open the position that way and now if we  see queen to h5 black has pawn to g6  and now bishop takes g6 hg6  queen g6 you run into a draw by  repetition  so king to h8 queen to h6 king to g8  queen to g6 this is your forced drawn  move  13.  okay let's say you're playing black and  you don't want to go for the force draw  you're trying to win this position  well after bishop to d3 you should play  pawn to c5  trying to get your space on the queen  side you're not necessarily going to try  and capture  but really just try and clamp down the  position where white is going to have  space on the king side black is going to  have space  on the queen side here knight to e2  and after c4 this bishop is not really  trapped don't worry  just bishop to f5 and now bishop takes  queen takes  and you'll see another queen exchange  and  here we end up in another similar  position  similar end game where white will have  the pressure  on the d5 pawn black will start to have  pressure  on the queen side after possibly running  their queens up the board  and this is probably one of the better  tries for black is to go for this end  game  which is equal only giving white  plus 0.2 or 0.3 depending on the depth  but that is pretty much the gist  of e4 e5 knight c3 that is almost  everything possible in the vienna for  those lines that i recommended  some of black's best tries as you can  see there's a lot of very  strong attacks some very natural looking  moves for black that are just complete  blunders  so if you are black and you play e5 this  is definitely an opening you're going to  want to study and look for those traps  make sure you're not going to fall for  them  and as white i would really recommend  this